Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy spent too much effort allowing arbitrary unconstrained
// text before SQL generation and then relied on ConstrainedSymbol for long
// multi-token expansions. On Spider, that often yields short but semantically
// under-specified SQL such as truncated "SELECT count" style outputs: syntax is
// valid, yet execution accuracy is poor. The revised strategy therefore makes
// the output aggressively SQL-centric.
//
// Concretely, if generation starts outside a constrained span, we immediately
// open one with OpenConstrainedSpan instead of waiting for an unconstrained
// chunk to discover "<<". This guarantees that almost the entire decoding budget
// is spent under the schema-aware SQL grammar. Once inside the span, decoding is
// token-by-token with ConstrainedStep rather than larger ConstrainedSymbol
// chunks. That gives tighter control over SQL structure, lets the parser steer
// every token choice, and reduces the tendency to stop after a bare aggregate or
// partial clause. To improve completion behavior, when the current constrained
// prefix is already complete we inspect its branching factor: if there are still
// multiple valid continuations, we keep decoding to encourage fuller queries;
// only when the parser says the prefix is complete and the number of valid next
// tokens is small do we close the span with CloseConstrainedSpan. We still never
// close merely because a sampled token looks like ">>"; closing happens only in
// a branch where parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) is known.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan branch: the helper enters constrained mode with
//     currentConstrainedOut := [], and parser.IsValidPrefix([]) is given by the
//     precondition.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan branch: we call it only after establishing
//     parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut); the helper exits
//     constrained mode, so the implication becomes vacuous.
//   - ConstrainedStep branch: the helper is called with a valid current prefix
//     and returns either EOS or a next token accepted by the parser. If EOS is
//     hit we break without changing constrained state; otherwise
//     AppendConstrainedToken preserves parser validity for the updated prefix.
//   - Any break branch leaves state unchanged, so the invariant is preserved.
//
// suffix:
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan branch: after opening, currentConstrainedOut is [],
//     so generated[|generated| - 0..] == [] holds trivially.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan branch: the helper resets currentConstrainedOut to [],
//     making the implication vacuous.
//   - ConstrainedStep + AppendConstrainedToken branch: AppendConstrainedToken
//     appends exactly the chosen constrained token to both generated and
//     currentConstrainedOut, so the constrained content remains the suffix of
//     generated.
//   - EOS/break branches do not modify generated or currentConstrainedOut, so
//     the invariant remains true.
//
// cost accounting:
//   - We maintain steps as the sole returned cost and set cost := steps at the
//     end.
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan and CloseConstrainedSpan each consume one step and we
//     increment steps by 1. ConstrainedStep also consumes one step and we
//     increment steps by 1 before any possible break or append. Query helpers
//     like ValidTokenCount do not consume cost.
//   - Every cost-bumping call is guarded by steps < maxSteps, so steps never
//     exceeds maxSteps, hence cost <= maxSteps on return.
//
// progress:
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan appends exactly one token ("<<") and we increment
//     steps by 1, preserving |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan appends exactly one token and again increments steps
//     by 1, preserving the bound.
//   - ConstrainedStep either returns EOS, in which case generated is unchanged
//     and only steps increases by 1, or returns one token that
//     AppendConstrainedToken appends exactly once while steps also increases by
//     1; both cases preserve the linear bound.
//   - Break branches exit immediately with the invariant already established.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
