Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The evaluation showed that the prior inner policy still under-generated SQL:
// it stayed syntactically valid, but often emitted very short prefixes such as
// "SELECT <id> ." and then stopped. The main problem was a brittle break policy:
// if the sampled token was not valid, or if the candidate list became sparse, we
// terminated immediately instead of recovering. For Spider, that is too fragile;
// the parser-valid SQL prefix must be actively steered toward a complete query.
//
// This revision changes the constrained strategy in three ways.
//
// First, outside the SQL span we no longer use large unconstrained chunks. We
// generate one token at a time with UnconstrainedStep until we see the "<<"
// opener or EOS. This makes entering the constrained SQL region more reliable
// and keeps the overall answer format simple.
//
// Second, inside the SQL span we use a stronger grammar-guided token policy.
// If the current SQL prefix is complete, we immediately close with ">>".
// Otherwise we compute top valid parser-approved candidates, heavily boost them,
// strongly penalize EOS, and sample. If the sample is invalid, we do not give up
// immediately: we deterministically fall back to the highest-logit valid
// candidate from the parser-approved set and append that token. This directly
// addresses the observed "valid but too short" failure mode by converting many
// near-misses into continued SQL growth instead of early stopping.
//
// Third, when the parser-approved candidate set is empty, we attempt recovery
// rather than stopping. We roll back the constrained content to the longest
// valid prefix and continue from there. This is conservative, keeps parser
// validity, and avoids being trapped by an over-committed partial SQL fragment.
//
// Overall, the strategy is now completion-seeking rather than stop-seeking:
// stay outside until "<<", then keep appending grammar-valid SQL tokens until
// the parser says the query is complete, and only then emit ">>".
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the outside-span branch, UnconstrainedStep either keeps us
// outside or appends "<<", after which we set currentConstrainedOut := []; the
// implication holds because parser.IsValidPrefix([]) is required. In the close
// branch, CloseConstrainedSpan exits the constrained region, so the implication
// is vacuous. In the sampled-append and fallback-append branches, we append only
// a token proven valid by IsTokenValidNext or chosen from TopValidCandidates, so
// AppendConstrainedToken preserves parser-validity. In the rollback branch,
// RollbackConstrainedSpan restores a parser-valid constrained prefix by helper
// contract intent. EOS and other break branches do not mutate constrained state.
